# Session scratch scripts.
#
# Reverse engineering produced ~340 one-off PowerShell probes in the project
# root (xb64.ps1, audit3.ps1, beat.ps1, ...). They are single-run diagnostics
# against one machine, they reference each other by cryptic name, and nothing
# in docs/ points at them. The 34 that the write-ups actually cite are kept
# under tools/ps/. Re-enable locally with:  git add -f <file>.ps1
/*.ps1

# Exceptions, and the reason each one earns it. The rule above is right -- a
# directory of 340 unmaintained probes is not documentation. But some scripts
# here are instruments rather than probes, they get re-run when a measurement has
# to be repeated, and leaving them untracked means a checkout loses the very tool
# the next measurement depends on. That happened: leak_slope.ps1 settled whether
# a multi-gigabyte growth was mine or the game's, and it existed only on one
# disk.
#
# So an exception is added here deliberately, with its reason, and the file is
# then held to the standard of the rest of the tree.
!leak_slope.ps1    # separates private-bytes growth by reads vs presents; the
                   # measurement that proved glReadPixels leaks 240 KB per call
!watchdog.ps1      # liveness sampling: CPU delta and log growth, since a
                   # crashed process keeps its pid and Responding=True
!rate.ps1          # measures the reporter's real period from file growth,
                   # instead of assuming one


# Permanent instruments, added 2026-09-30: the ignore rule above exists for
# the 340 one-shot probes, but these are the repeatable harnesses and they
# were never in the repository because of it.
!run_v1.ps1
!gline.ps1
